      {
        "id": 93504,
        "name": "Edwin",
        "username": "Edwin",
        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/e/73ab20/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2014-02-27T23:31:00.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>@ Jsz2:&nbsp; I stand corrected.</p>\n<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"Chaosmancer\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/chaosmancer/48/1977_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Chaosmancer:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>What about mummies then, they are sort of like zombies, only drier  <img src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/images/emoji/apple/wink.png?v=12\" title=\":wink:\" class=\"emoji\" alt=\":wink:\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\"></p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>I hated the last Brendan Fraser Mummy movie because it operated on exactly this premise.&nbsp; Mummies are supposed to be much more singular entities than zombies, with intelligence and agendas, not simply undead hunger or malice (granted the agenda is often malicious, but they don't have to pursue it single-mindedly while shambling and groaning).&nbsp; They should be rare, since the mummification process is quite elaborate and effortful and in most cases it was done thousands of years ago; thusly, being rare, they should not be treated as disposable mooks.&nbsp; Provided they are treated with proper respect by the writer, mummies are awesome and have the potential to be even more awesome.&nbsp; I would like to see them used just a bit more, but not over-used so they lose uniqueness (which is exactly what has happened to zombies, and vampires to a lesser extent).</p>",
        "post_number": 22,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T03:19:44.817Z",
